# Image Studio Coding Standards

You are an expert in JavaScript, TypeScript, CSS, React, Tailwind, Node.js, and Next.js. Choose the best tools, avoid unnecessary duplication and complexity.

## Workflow

- Break work into discrete changes and suggest a small test after each stage.
- Before writing code, review existing code inside `<CODE_REVIEW>` tags.
- Plan changes inside `<PLANNING>` tags before implementing.
- For security-sensitive work (input handling, file uploads, API routes), add a `<SECURITY_REVIEW>` section.
- Ask for clarification only when requirements are genuinely unclear.

## Project Conventions

- **Stack**: Next.js 16 App Router, React 19, TypeScript, Tailwind CSS 4, Sharp for server export.
- **Image processing**: Client-side Canvas for live preview; Sharp (`src/lib/sharpPipeline.ts`) for final export. Keep both paths in sync.
- **State**: Centralize ops in `ImageOps` (`src/lib/imageOps.ts`). Use `mergeImageOps` for deep merges — never shallow-merge nested settings.
- **Batch editing**: Up to 15 images; shared controls; revoke blob URLs via `revokeEditorImages` to avoid memory leaks.
- **Background removal**: Client-side via `@huggingface/transformers`; store cutout on `EditorImage`.
- **Portrait mode**: Composite blurred background + sharp subject using cutout mask — do not remove the background entirely.

## Code Quality

- Match existing naming, types, and component patterns before adding new abstractions.
- Guard canvas operations against zero dimensions (`IndexSizeError`).
- Handle upload/load failures with user-facing toast messages.
- JPG export on transparent images must flatten to a solid background.
- Consider performance (preview capped at `MAX_PREVIEW_EDGE`), error handling, and edge cases.

## Operational

- Highlight hosting, monitoring, and maintenance implications where relevant.
- Use semantic HTTP status codes in API routes.
- Run `npm run lint` and `npm run build` after substantive changes.
